        <section id="v2-expansion">
          <h2>6. v2.0 Runtime Expansion (Implemented Subsystems)</h2>
          <p>
            Beyond the measured v1.0 core, Cockpit v2.0 expands <code>orchestrator-runtime</code> to <strong>52 Rust modules</strong> (~68,000 LOC across 67 Rust files), backed by <strong>216 Rust unit tests</strong>. We report these subsystems as implemented architecture rather than claiming unmeasured end-to-end token savings:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Low-Overhead MCP Protocol:</strong> <em>MCP-Zero lazy schema</em> (<code>COCKPIT_MCP_MODE=full|lazy|code</code>) to avoid bloating tool declarations, compact tabular <strong>TOON</strong> outputs, and batched multi-call execution via <code>cockpit_batch</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Speculative Caching &amp; Persistent Context Maps:</strong> <strong>PASTE Speculative Cache</strong> for frequent read/query reuse and <strong>Text Cartridge</strong> (<code>.cockpit/cartridge.md</code>) for KV-cache-friendly compressed repository orientation.</li>
            <li><strong>On-Demand Semantics &amp; Selective Execution:</strong> <strong>LSP JSON-RPC</strong> on-demand client (<code>rust-analyzer</code>, <code>luau-lsp</code>, <code>basedpyright</code>, <code>ts-ls</code>), <strong>RTS</strong> selective test execution scoped to touched modules, <strong>Compiled AI</strong> deterministic 0-token flows, and <code>docs_lookup</code> grounded directly in installed <code>Cargo.lock</code> / <code>node_modules</code> versions.</li>
            <li><strong>Runtime Guards &amp; Review Prioritization:</strong> <strong>MintMCP Guard</strong> (risk scoring + anti-prompt-injection scanning), <strong>MidStreamGuard</strong> and <strong>Termination Lag Guard</strong> for anomalous stream/process abort, <strong>Diff Radar</strong> risk-ranked hunk prioritization, and cross-process <strong>Resource Guard FIFO</strong> (<code>sysinfo</code> + OS file lock).</li>
            <li><strong>Zero-Vision Harnesses &amp; Distributed Control:</strong> Win32 UIA-first desktop automation, CPU Blender/Roblox/FFmpeg harnesses, and local-first multi-device orchestration (<strong>Iroh QUIC</strong>, <strong>Tailscale</strong>, userspace <strong><code>wgproxyrs</code></strong>, and <strong>Kotlin Android</strong>). Detailed in our two companion reports: <a href="/en/lab/cockpit-zero-vision/">Zero-Vision Automation ↗</a> and <a href="/en/lab/cockpit-distributed/">Multi-Device Orchestration ↗</a>.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="validity">
          <h2>7. Threats to Validity &amp; 8. Conclusion</h2>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Measured vs. Implemented Scope:</strong> Tables 1–2 come from the deterministic v1.0 benchmark harness; Section 6 subsystems are implemented and covered by unit invariants across 216 Rust tests, with dedicated E2E ablations planned as future work.</li>
            <li><strong>Offline Token Proxy vs. Vendor Tokenizers:</strong> <code>ceil(chars / 4)</code> may differ by ±5–10% from provider-specific BPE tokenizers, while 69%–97% compression ratios remain structurally stable because byte volume drops equivalently.</li>
            <li><strong>Controlled E2E Solve-Rate Ablation:</strong> Deterministic ablations isolate compression, loop detection, and routing latency with zero variance; Section 5.3 reports observational telemetry rather than a randomized SWE-bench trial (planned future work).</li>
            <li><strong>Platform &amp; Source Scope:</strong> Cockpit targets Windows desktop workflows (with Linux runtime/peer support) and resides in a private repository (<code>agent-orchestrator</code>).</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

